label("$D_{1}$",(0,1),W);
label("$\alpha$",A,NE);
label("$\beta$",B,SW);
label("$\gamma$",C,NW);
label("$\delta$",D,S);
label("$a_{1}$",(0,1),N);
label("$b_{1}$",(3,1),W);
label("$c_{1}$",(2.5,2),W);
label("$d_{1}$",(0,1),S);
[/asy]

Let $a, b, c$ and $d$ be the areas of triangles $A B C, B C D, C D A$ and $D A B$, respectively. Then $a_{1}=\frac{1}{2} A A_{1} \cdot A D_{1} \sin \alpha$, $b_{1}=\frac{1}{2} B A_{1} \cdot B B_{1} \sin \beta$, $c_{1}=\frac{1}{2} B B_{1} \cdot B C_{1} \sin \gamma$ and $d_{1}=\frac{1}{2} C C_{1} \cdot C D_{1} \sin \delta$. Also, $a=\frac{1}{2} A B \cdot B C \sin \beta$, $b=\frac{1}{2} B C \cdot C D \sin \gamma$, $c=\frac{1}{2} C D \cdot D A \sin \delta$ and $d=\frac{1}{2} D A \cdot A B \sin \alpha$.

Thus, $a_{1}=\frac{A A_{1}}{A B} \cdot \frac{A D_{1}}{D A} \cdot d$, $b_{1}=\frac{B A_{1}}{A B} \cdot \frac{B B_{1}}{B C} \cdot a$, $c_{1}=\frac{B B_{1}}{B C} \cdot \frac{B C_{1}}{C D} \cdot b$ and $d_{1}=\frac{C C_{1}}{C D} \cdot \frac{C D_{1}}{D A} \cdot c$.

Let $x=\frac{A A_{1}}{A B}, y=\frac{B B_{1}}{B C}, z=\frac{C C_{1}}{C D}$ and $w=\frac{D D_{1}}{D A}$. Then $0 \leq x, y, z, w \leq 1$, and $a_{1}=\frac{x}{1-w} d, b_{1}=\frac{1-x}{1-y} a, c_{1}=\frac{y}{1-z} b$ and $d_{1}=\frac{1-z}{1-w} c$. We wish to prove that $2\left(\frac{x}{1-w} d+\frac{1-x}{1-y} a\right) \geq S_{1}$, assuming that $\frac{x}{1-w} d \leq \frac{1-x}{1-y} a \leq \frac{y}{1-z} b \leq \frac{1-z}{1-w} c$.

We have $S_{1}=a+b+c+d-a_{1}-b_{1}-c_{1}-d_{1}=a+b+c+d-\frac{x}{1-w} d-\frac{1-x}{1-y} a-\frac{y}{1-z} b-\frac{1-z}{1-w} c=\frac{1-x}{1-y} a+\frac{1-z}{1-w} c-\frac{y}{1-z} b-\frac{x}{1-w} d$. Thus, $2\left(\frac{x}{1-w} d+\frac{1-x}{1-y} a\right) \geq S_{1}$ is equivalent to $3\left(\frac{x}{1-w} d+\frac{1-x}{1-y} a\right) \geq \frac{1-z}{1-w} c+\frac{y}{1-z} b$.

We can write $a_{1}=\frac{x}{1-w} d \leq \frac{1-x}{1-y} a=b_{1}$ as $x(1-y) d \leq (1-x)(1-w) a$. Since $a_{1} \leq c_{1} \leq d_{1}$, we have $\frac{x}{1-w} d \leq \frac{y}{1-z} b \leq \frac{1-z}{1-w} c$. Thus, $\frac{x}{1-w} d \leq \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{y}{1-z} b+\frac{1-z}{1-w} c\right)$.

Similarly, we can write $b_{1}=\frac{1-x}{1-y} a \leq \frac{y}{1-z} b \leq \frac{1-z}{1-w} c$ as $(1-x)(1-z) a \leq y(1-y) b$. Since $b_{1} \leq c_{1} \leq d_{1}$, we have $\frac{1-x}{1-y} a \leq \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{y}{1-z} b+\frac{1-z}{1-w} c\right)$.

Thus, we have $3\left(\frac{x}{1-w} d+\frac{1-x}{1-y} a\right) \geq 3\left(\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{y}{1-z} b+\frac{1-z}{1-w} c\right)\right)=\frac{3}{2}\left(\frac{y}{1-z} b+\frac{1-z}{1-w} c\right) \geq \frac{1-z}{1-w} c+\frac{y}{1-z} b$. Therefore, $2\left(\frac{x}{1-w} d+\frac{1-x}{1-y} a\right) \geq S_{1}$, so $2 S \geq S_{1}$.
